      "version": 2451,
      "versionNonce": 648529591,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779080124813,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) User enter the src, destination and request the ride type and fare for a trip.\n2) The RideTypeAndFare Service use google or some external APIs to get ETA.\n3) FareRecommendation system use src, destination, rideType and ETA to determine the actual fare or fare range.\n4) The response with fare and eta details is shown at app screen (synchronous call, but could be turned into asynchronous putting everything in queue and then using webhook to show the details on the screen)\n5) Ride confirmation request is pushed into the message queue, to have high throughput.\n6) Book Ride Service consumes the event from queue and works to save rideDetails, confirm driver, send notification back to user.\n7) The requested ride details is saved as \"RideRequested\" in db with null driverId.\n8) Retrieve driver in proximity using geohashed storage like Redis.\n9) Loop in through sorted driversList and for each driver send a notification and put a redis lock on the driverId, so no other concurrent request sends the notification to driver while previous request is already sent.\nTTL can be for 5 secs and if no confirmation is received the notif is sent to next driver. So while lock is held in redis for any driver no other request is sent to them.\n10) Webhook sends the notification on driver UI screen.\n11) Now, as driver accepts or decline the request, Ride confirmation service is a synchronous call takes that request.\n12) Updates the entry in the table as \"Booking Confirmed\" and loop ends or break if accepted and if not the driver loop continues.\n13) Driver location updates are a frequent call through location update api, the event is sent to kafka.\n14 & 15) Location Update service consumes that event and updates the geohashed storage redis since it can handle higher tps.\n16) OTP Verification service verifies the otp coming from the request and stored in the RideBooking table upon ride is accepted.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) User enter the src, destination and request the ride type and fare for a trip.\n2) The RideTypeAndFare Service use google or some external APIs to get ETA.\n3) FareRecommendation system use src, destination, rideType and ETA to determine the actual fare or fare range.\n4) The response with fare and eta details is shown at app screen (synchronous call, but could be turned into asynchronous putting everything in queue and then using webhook to show the details on the screen)\n5) Ride confirmation request is pushed into the message queue, to have high throughput.\n6) Book Ride Service consumes the event from queue and works to save rideDetails, confirm driver, send notification back to user.\n7) The requested ride details is saved as \"RideRequested\" in db with null driverId.\n8) Retrieve driver in proximity using geohashed storage like Redis.\n9) Loop in through sorted driversList and for each driver send a notification and put a redis lock on the driverId, so no other concurrent request sends the notification to driver while previous request is already sent.\nTTL can be for 5 secs and if no confirmation is received the notif is sent to next driver. So while lock is held in redis for any driver no other request is sent to them.\n10) Webhook sends the notification on driver UI screen.\n11) Now, as driver accepts or decline the request, Ride confirmation service is a synchronous call takes that request.\n12) Updates the entry in the table as \"Booking Confirmed\" and loop ends or break if accepted and if not the driver loop continues.\n13) Driver location updates are a frequent call through location update api, the event is sent to kafka.\n14 & 15) Location Update service consumes that event and updates the geohashed storage redis since it can handle higher tps.\n16) OTP Verification service verifies the otp coming from the request and stored in the RideBooking table upon ride is accepted.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
